Webpack config or update this line in the host.\n\n2.  **Shared Dependencies (Removed):** The original `host/webpack.config.js` had a `shared` section for `vue`, enforcing a specific version.  This has been removed.  For production, it''s generally a good practice to share dependencies between host and remote applications to avoid loading the same library multiple times.  If both host and remote use Vue, consider re-adding a `shared` configuration like this to `host/webpack.config.js`:\n\n    ```javascript\n    shared: {\n      vue: {\n        singleton: true,\n        requiredVersion: \"^3.0.0\", // Or whatever version you''re using\n      },\n    },\n    ```\n\n    You will likely also want to share Vue in `remote/webpack.config.js`:\n\n    ```javascript\n    new ModuleFederationPlugin({\n      // ... other config\n      shared: {\n        vue: {\n          singleton: true,\n          requiredVersion: \"^3.0.0\", // Match the host''s version\n        },\n      },\n    }),\n    ```\n\n3.  **Error Handling:**  The `RemoteWrapper.vue` component uses `Suspense` for a loading state, but it lacks error handling.  If the remote component fails to load (e.g., network error, remote server down), the application will hang on the \"Загрузка...\" message.  Add an error state to provide a more user-friendly experience:\n\n    ```vue\n    <template>\n      <Suspense>\n        <component :is=\"remoteComponent\" />\n        <template #fallback>\n          <div>Загрузка...</div>\n        </template>\n      </Suspense>\n      <div v-if=\"error\" style=\"color: red;\">Ошибка загрузки компонента: {{ error }}</div>\n    </template>\n\n    <script setup>\n    import { ref } from ''vue'';\n\n    const remoteComponent = ref(null);\n    const error = ref(null); // Add an error ref\n\n    const load = async () => {\n      try {\n        const module = await import(\"remote/App\");\n        remoteComponent.value = module.default;\n      } catch (e) {\n        error.value = e.message || ''Unknown error''; // Capture the error\n      }\n    };\n\n    load();\n    </script>\n    ```\n\n4.  **Clarity in `webpack.config.js`:** In `remote/webpack.config.js` the `isRemoteDev` variable is declared but not used. This could be removed for better clarity.\n\n5.  **HTML Template Deduplication:** The HTML template in `remote/webpack.config.js` is almost identical to a standard Vue project''s `index.html`. Consider using a separate `index.html` file for the remote application instead of embedding it in `templateContent`.  This simplifies the Webpack configuration and makes it easier to manage the HTML.\n\n**To Test:**\n\n1.  Ensure both `host` and `remote` projects have their dependencies installed (`npm install`).\n2.  Start the development server for the `remote` application (e.g., `npm run serve` or the equivalent command defined in its `package.json`). Make sure it runs on port 3000.\n3.  Start the development server for the `host` application.\n4.  Access the host application in your browser. You should see content from both the host and the remote applications rendered together.\n\nBy addressing the suggestions above (port consistency, shared dependencies, error handling, and code clarity), you''ll have a more robust and maintainable Module Federation setup.","tree":{"sha":"2babb2368fc76e46571e48f4ed3c3d5c694b349d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ddenysov/canope/git/trees/2babb2368fc76e46571e48f4ed3c3d5c694b349d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ddenysov/canope/git/commits/28d584a4a746e24217b25974a950585d73e28fa1","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3142325?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ddenysov/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ddenysov/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ddenysov/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dd