 gateway''s file) to ensure it exists.  For example, in `gateway/docker-compose.yml`, you could define the network as:\n\n    ```yaml\n    networks:\n      intranet:\n        driver: bridge\n    ```\n\n    This way, the network will be automatically created when you bring up the gateway service.\n\n*   **Makefile:**  The `gateway/Makefile` is well-structured for basic operations.  You might add a `logs` command to follow the Traefik logs for easier debugging:\n\n    ```makefile\n    logs:\n    \tdocker compose logs -f traefik\n    ```\n\n*   **Error Handling:** Consider adding error handling or checks within the `Makefile` commands to improve robustness. For instance, checking if commands succeed or providing more informative messages.\n\n*   **Comments:** While the comments are present, consider adding more comments to explain complex configurations, especially within the Traefik static configuration file, explaining the purpose of each setting.\n\n**Example of secure dashboard (in `gateway/docker-compose.yml`):**\n\nTo secure the dashboard, you''d need to configure an HTTP Basic Auth middleware and apply it to the dashboard route.  Here''s how you''d modify `gateway/docker-compose.yml` (and you would remove `--api.insecure=true`):\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n  traefik:\n    image: traefik:v2.11\n    command:\n      - \"--api.dashboard=true\"\n      - \"--providers.docker=true\"\n      - \"--entrypoints.web.address=:8000\"\n      - \"--entrypoints.traefik.address=:8080\" # Add this for secure dashboard access\n    ports:\n      - \"8000:8000\"\n      - \"8081:8080\"\n    networks:\n      - intranet\n    volumes:\n      - \"/var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock:ro\"\n      - \"./config:/etc/traefik\" # Mount a config directory\n    labels:\n      - \"traefik.http.routers.traefik.rule=Host(`traefik.localhost`)\" # Route to dashboard\n      - \"traefik.http.routers.traefik.service=api@internal\"\n      - \"traefik.http.routers.traefik.entrypoints=traefik\" # Use the secure entrypoint\n      - \"traefik.http.routers.traefik.middlewares=traefik-auth\" # Apply authentication\n      - \"traefik.http.middlewares.traefik-auth.basicauth.users=admin:$$apr1$$lKVd8t8.$$oF7.e8oM8Q64N6n5K.b3g1\" # Replace with actual hashed credentials\n\nnetworks:\n  intranet:\n    external: true\n```\n\nYou''d also need to modify `gateway/config/traefik_static.yml` to remove the insecure API access and configure the `traefik` entrypoint:\n\n```yaml\nentryPoints:\n  web:\n    address: \":8000\"\n  traefik: # Add this entrypoint for dashboard access\n    address: \":8080\"\n\n# No more api section\n```\n\nRemember to replace the dummy credentials in the `docker-compose.yml` with your actual hashed credentials.  You can generate the hash using `htpasswd`. Now, the dashboard would be accessible on `traefik.localhost:8081` (assuming you''ve configured your hosts file to resolve `traefik.localhost` to your Docker host''s IP).","tree":{"sha":"db8fa5479a46420e8a45cef7c60cdcadb8a6c6c4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ddenysov/canope/git/trees/db8fa5479a46420e8a45cef7c60cdcadb8a6c6c4"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ddenysov/canope/git/commits/13f13b77aed2c48f54eae71b6146be217534de10","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":null,"html_url":"https://github.com/ddenysov/canope/commit/13f13b77aed2c48f54eae71b6146be217534de10","node_id":"C_kwDOO2zci9oAKDEzZjEzYjc3YWVkMmM0OGY1NGVhZTcxYjYxNDZiZTIxNzUzNGRlMTA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/ddenysov/canope/commit/0fe78150c5db1912a7aa05b9a3fcae697c14c482","sha":"0fe78150c5db1912a7aa05b9a3fcae697c14c482","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ddenysov/canope/commits/0fe78150c5db1912a7aa05b9a3fcae697c14c482"}],"sha":"13f13b77aed2c48f54eae71b6146be217534de10","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ddenysov/canope/commits/13f13b77aed2c48f54eae71b6146be217534de10"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ddenysov/canope/commits/0fe78150c5db1912a7aa05b9a3fcae697c14c482/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2025-06-06T06:54:35Z","email":"dmytro.denysov@danads.se","name":"Dmytro Denysov"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2025-06-06T06:54:35Z","email":"dmytro.denysov@danads.se","name":"Dmytro Denysov"},"message":"This set of changes introduces a new \"user-service\" into the project, including:\n\n*   **Core Logic:** Defines domain models (`Ping`, `PingId`), events (`PingCreated`), and application-level commands and handlers (`PingCommand`, `PingCommandHandler`).\n*   **Delivery Mechanism:** Sets up an HTTP endpoint (`/`) that triggers the `PingCommand` via a controller (`IndexController`).\n*   **Infrastructure:** Includes Doctrine migrations for creating database tables (`event_store`, `read_model_users`, `outbox`) and configurations for database access (`migrations.yaml`, `migrations-db.php`).\n*   **Event Handling:** Implements an event handler (`PingCreatedEventHandler`) that reacts to `PingCreated` events by updating a read model (`read_model_users`).\n*   **RoadRunner Integration:** Configures RoadRunner for handling HTTP requests and managing workers (`.rr.yaml`, `workers/worker.php`).  It also sets up a separate worker (`workers/outbox.php`) to implement the transactional outbox pattern, ensuring messages are reliably published.\n*   **Dependency Management:**  A `composer.json` file defines the project''s dependencies, including a local \"core\" library and various Symfony and Spiral packages.\n*   **Container Configuration:**  Uses Symfony''s Dependency Injection component to manage service configurations (`config/services.yaml`).\n*   **Routing:**  Defines the route for the HTTP endpoint (`config/routes.yaml`).\n*   **Dockerization:** Includes a `docker-compose.yml` file for running the service within a Docker container.\n*   **Development Support:** Provides a `Makefile` for common tasks like starting/stopping the service, running migrations, and accessing the container''s shell. Also includes `.env` and `.gitignore` files for environment configuration and ignoring versioned files.\n\n**Key Improvements and Observations:**\n\n*   **Event Sourcing and CQRS:** The service appears to be implementing a basic form of Event Sourcing with CQRS. Events (`PingCreated`) are recorded in an `event_store`, and changes are reflected in read models (`read_model_users`). Commands (`PingCommand`) trigger domain logic changes.\n*   **Transactional Outbox:**  The `outbox` table and related worker implement the transactional outbox pattern, which is crucial for ensuring reliable message delivery in distributed systems. This pattern ensures that messages are only marked as published if they are successfully sent to the message broker.\n*   **RoadRunner Integration:**  The use of RoadRunner suggests a focus on performance and efficient resource utilization.\n*   **Symfony Framework:** The project leverages Symfony components for routing, dependency injection, and potentially other features, indicating a structured and maintainable codebase.\n*   **Database Migrations:** Doctrine Migrations are used, which is good practice for managing database schema changes.\n*   **Docker Setup:** The inclusion of `docker-compose.yml` simplifies local development and deployment.\n\n**Potential Improvement Areas (Without Deeper Code Inspection):**\n\n*   **Missing Message Broker:** The `outbox.php` worker attempts to publish messages, but the code doesn''t explicitly configure or use a message broker (like RabbitMQ, Kafka, etc.). You''ll need to integrate a message broker to actually deliver the messages.\n*   **Eventual Consistency:**  Since this architecture seems to employ CQRS, remember that updates to the read model (`read_model_users`) will be eventually consistent with the event store.  Consider implications for data visibility in the application.\n*   **Error Handling:** The `outbox.php` worker has a basic try/catch block, but consider adding more robust error handling and retry mechanisms for message publishing.\n*   **Testing:** There are no tests included in these changes.  Adding unit and integration tests will be crucial.\n*   **Configuration:** Some configurations, like the message broker connection details, are likely missing and need to be added.\n*   **DataStore Abstraction:** The `DataStoreInterface` is used, which is good for decoupling.  However, the specific implementation (e.g., Doctrine DBAL) might be hardcoded or configured elsewhere, which should be checked.\n*   **Logging:**  Logging is used, but ensure that the log messages contain sufficient context and are formatted consistently.\n\n**To provide more specific feedback, I would need to see the code for:**\n\n*   The `Zinc\\Core` library, particularly the implementations of `CommandBusInterface`, `DataStoreInterface`, `MessagePublisherInterface`, `Logger`, `AbstractAggregateRoot`, and related interfaces/classes.\n*   Any configuration files related to the message broker.\n*   Any service definitions or bootstrapping logic that isn''t directly in the provided files.\n\nOverall, this looks like a well-structured start to a new service, incorporating modern architectural patterns and technologies.  The key next steps are to integrate a message broker, add comprehensive tests, and refine error handling.","tree":{"sha":"cb4b47510c8c230acbe61eda0978a65a5c8e0968","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ddenysov/canope/git/trees/cb4b47510c8c230acbe61eda0978a65a5c8e0968"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ddenysov/canope/git/commits/0fe78150c5db1912a7aa05b9a3fcae697c14c482","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":null,"html_url":"https://github.com/ddenysov/canope/commit/0fe78150c5db1912a7aa05b9a3fcae697c14c482","node_id":"C_kwDOO2zci9oAKDBmZTc4MTUwYzVkYjE5MTJhN2FhMDViOWEzZmNhZTY5N2MxNGM0ODI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/ddenysov/canope/commit/9fed00cb24b34adcf68b2865302a000637b3c2bc","sha":"9fed00cb24b34adcf68b2865302a000637b3c2bc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ddenysov/canope/commits/9fed00cb24b34adcf68b2865302a000637b3c2bc"}],"sha":"0fe78150c5db1912a7aa05b9a3fcae697c14c482","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ddenysov/canope/commits/0fe78150c5db1912a7aa05b9a3fcae697c14c482"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ddenysov/canope/commits/9fed00cb24b34adcf68b2865302a000637b3c2bc/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2025-06-06T06:30:22Z","email":"dmytro.denysov@danads.se","name":"Dmytro Denysov"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2025-06-06T06:30:22Z","email":"dmytro.denysov@danads.se","name":"Dmytro Denysov"},"message":"The code introduces a Domain-Driven Design (DDD) structure in a PHP project, focusing on core functionalities like command handling, event sourcing, and data persistence. It uses RoadRunner for handling HTTP and job requests, Symfony Messenger for message handling, and PDO for database interactions. The code includes interfaces and abstract classes for entities, aggregates, repositories, commands, and events, promoting a clear separation of concerns and testability.\n\nHere''s a breakdown of the key components and potential improvements:\n\n**Key Components:**\n\n*   **Domain Layer:** Defines core concepts like Aggregates (`AbstractAggregateRoot`), Entities (`AbstractEntity`), Value Objects (`Uuid`, `AbstractValue`), Commands (`CommandInterface`), and Events (`AbstractEvent`). This layer is framework-agnostic and encapsulates business logic.\n*   **Infrastructure Layer:** Implements interfaces from the domain layer using specific technologies. For example, `PdoDataStore` provides a database implementation using PDO, and `InMemoryCommandBus` offers an in-memory command bus for testing.\n*   **Application Layer:** The `JobsWorker` and `HttpWorker` classes handle incoming requests from RoadRunner, dispatching commands or processing HTTP requests through the Symfony kernel.\n*   **Command Handling:** Uses a command bus pattern (`InMemoryCommandBus`, `MessengerCommandBus`) to route commands to their respective handlers.  Decorators (`EventStoreDecorator`, `RetryDecorator`, `EventBusDecorator`) enhance command handlers with features like event storage, retry mechanisms, and event publishing.\n*   **Event Sourcing:** `AbstractAggregateRoot` tracks changes as events, allowing reconstruction of aggregate state from historical events.  The `EventStoreRepository` persists these events.\n*   **Message Handling:** Leverages Symfony Messenger for both commands and events, with a custom serializer (`CloudEventSerializer`) for encoding messages as CloudEvents.  A RoadRunner transport (`RoadRunnerTransport`, `RoadRunnerFactory`) integrates Messenger with RoadRunner''s queue system.\n*   **Data Persistence:**  Provides a `DataStoreInterface` for database interactions, with a PDO implementation (`PdoDataStore`). Includes schema management through `SqlSchemaTool`.\n\n**Areas for Improvement and Suggestions:**\n\n1.  **Configuration and Dependency Injection:** The current setup seems to rely heavily on autowiring and manual instantiation within the `JobsWorker` and `HttpWorker`.  Consider using Symfony''s dependency injection more explicitly (e.g., through service definitions in `services.yaml`) to improve maintainability and testability.  For instance, the database connection details are hardcoded in `PdoDataStore`. These should be configurable.\n\n2.  **Error Handling:**  While there are `try...catch` blocks in several places (e.g., `JobsWorker::serve`, `RetryDecorator`), a more consistent and centralized error handling strategy could be beneficial.  Consider using Symfony''s exception handling mechanisms or introducing a dedicated error handling component.  The exception message in the `RetryDecorator` only logs a generic message. It should log the actual exception message for better debugging.\n\n3.  **Event Storage:** The `EventStoreDecorator` currently inserts dummy data into `event_store` and `outbox`.  This needs to be replaced with the logic to persist the actual events from the command handler''s result and populate the `outbox` correctly (if you''re using the outbox pattern).\n\n4.  **Outbox Pattern:**  The code includes an `outbox` table, suggesting an intention to implement the Outbox pattern for reliable event publishing. However, the logic to actually publish messages from the outbox to a message broker (like RabbitMQ or Kafka) is missing. You''ll need a separate process (e.g., a cron job or a dedicated worker) to read from the outbox table and publish messages using a `MessagePublisherInterface` implementation for your chosen broker.\n\n5.  **Logging:**  Logging is done through a custom `Logger` class that acts as a facade for a PSR-3 logger.  While functional, consider directly using Symfony''s logging configuration and the `Psr\\Log\\LoggerInterface` in your services for better integration with the framework.\n\n6.  **Testing:**  The provided tests (`CommandHandlerTest`) are a good start, but you''ll need more comprehensive tests, including integration tests that cover interactions between different components (e.g., command handling, event persistence, and event publishing).\n\n7.  **RoadRunner Configuration:**  The `.rr.yaml` file, which is essential for configuring RoadRunner, is missing from the provided diff.  This file should define the HTTP and job services, including the command for starting the PHP worker.  For example:\n\n    ```yaml\n    server:\n        command: \"php worker.php\" # Assuming your dispatcher is in worker.php\n        relay: tcp://127.0.0.1:6001\n        pool:\n            num_workers: 4\n            max_jobs: 64\n\n    http:\n        address: 0.0.0.0:8080\n        middleware:\n            - gzip\n            - headers\n        headers:\n            allowed_origins: [\"*\"]\n            expose: [\"X-Debug-Token\", \"X-Debug-Token-Link\"]\n            max_age: 31536000\n            strict_transport_security: \"max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains\"\n        uploads:\n            dir: \"/tmp\"\n            forbid: [\".php\", \".exe\", \".bat\"]\n\n    jobs:\n      consume: [\"default\"]\n      pipelines:\n        default:\n          driver: memory\n          prefetch: 10\n    ```\n\n8.  **Jobs Pipeline**: In `libs/core/src/Messaging/Symfony/RoadRunner/RoadRunnerTransport.php`, the queue name `demo-queue` is hardcoded. This should be configurable and probably injected.\n\n9.  **Cloud Event Serializer**: In `libs/core/src/Messaging/Symfony/Serializer/CloudEventSerializer.php`, the `source` attribute is hardcoded as `''TBD''` and the extension handling logic is commented out. This should be completed and made configurable.  Also, consider using a dedicated library for CloudEvents (there are several available) to handle the serialization and deserialization more robustly.\n\n10. **Retry Mechanism**: The `RetryDecorator` retries on *any* exception.  Consider adding more specific exception handling to retry only on transient errors (e.g., database connection issues, temporary network problems) and not on application-level errors that are unlikely to be resolved by retrying (e.g., invalid input).\n\n**Example Improvements (Illustrative):**\n\n**1. Configuration (using Symfony''s Dependency Injection):**\n\nIn `libs/core/src/Container/Symfony/SymfonyCoreBundle.php`, you can register services and configure them.  For instance, to make database connection details configurable, add parameters to your `services.yaml` (or in the bundle''s `loadExtension` method):\n\n```yaml\nparameters:\n    database_dsn: ''sqlite:/app/var/demo.db'' # Or a DSN for MySQL, PostgreSQL, etc.\n\nservices:\n    # ... other service definitions ...\n    Zinc\\Core\\DataStore\\Bridge\\PDO\\PdoDataStore:\n        arguments:\n            $dialect: ''@Zinc\\Core\\DataStore\\Bridge\\PDO\\Dialect\\SqliteDialect''\n            $dsn: ''%database_dsn%''\n        autowire: true\n        public: true\n```\n\nThen, modify the `PdoDataStore` constructor:\n\n```php\npublic function __construct(\n    private Dialect $dialect = new SqliteDialect(),\n    private string $dsn, // Inject the DSN\n    private StatementCache $stmtCache = new StatementCache(),\n    private SqlCriteriaCompiler $compiler = new SqlCriteriaCompiler(),\n) {\n    $this->pdo = new \\PDO($this->dsn, null, null, [ // Use injected DSN\n        \\PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE => \\PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION,\n        \\PDO::ATTR_DEFAULT_FETCH_MODE => \\PDO::FETCH_ASSOC,\n    ]);\n}\n```\n\n**2. Improved Retry Decorator:**\n\n```php\nclass RetryDecorator implements CommandHandlerInterface\n{\n    // ... (other code)\n\n    public function __invoke(CommandInterface $command)\n    {\n        // ...\n        } catch (\\Throwable $e) {\n            Logger::error(sprintf(''Command handler execution failed: %s'', $e->getMessage())); // Log actual exception\n            $attempt++;\n\n            if ($attempt >= $this->maxAttempts) {\n                throw $e;\n            }\n\n            if (!($e instanceof TransientException)) { // Example: Only retry on a custom TransientException\n                throw $e;\n            }\n\n            \\usleep($delay * 1000);\n            $delay += $this->stepMs;\n            // ...\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n(You''d also need to define or use an existing `TransientException` class).\n\n**3. Event Storage and Outbox:**\n\nIn `EventStoreDecorator`, you need to extract the event data from the command handler result (assuming it returns an event or a collection of events) and insert it into the `event_store` table. You also need to create records in the `outbox` table for each event that needs to be published.  This requires adapting the table schema in the SQL migrations to match your event structure.\n\n**Further Steps:**\n\n*   Implement a mechanism to read from the `outbox` table and publish messages to a message broker. This could involve a dedicated worker process or a scheduled task.  You''ll need a `MessagePublisherInterface` implementation for your chosen broker (e.g., RabbitMQ, Kafka).\n*   Complete the CloudEvents integration by providing a proper source and trace/correlation ID handling in the `CloudEventSerializer`.\n*   Add more comprehensive tests, including integration tests.\n*   Refactor for clarity and maintainability, breaking down large classes into smaller, more focused ones.  Consider creating DTOs (Data Transfer Objects) for passing data between layers.","tree":{"sha":"21d72da543567a658d2e5bd429113ab51db72ea7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ddenysov/canope/git/trees/21d72da543567a658d2e5bd429113ab51db72ea7"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ddenysov/canope/git/commits/9fed00cb24b34adcf68b2865302a000637b3c2bc","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":null,"html_url":"https://github.com/ddenysov/canope/commit/9fed00cb24b34adcf68b2865302a000637b3c2bc","node_id":"C_kwDOO2zci9oAKDlmZWQwMGNiMjRiMzRhZGNmNjhiMjg2NTMwMmEwMDA2MzdiM2MyYmM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/ddenysov/canope/commit/0c9dcbf7a6c4e3d7d715f024aee93a9961219c4a","sha":"0c9dcbf7a6c4e3d7d715f024aee93a9961219c4a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ddenysov/canope/commits/0c9dcbf7a6c4e3d7d715f024aee93a9961219c4a"}],"sha":"9fed00cb24b34adcf68b2865302a000637b3c2bc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ddenysov/canope/commits/9fed00cb24b34adcf68b2865302a000637b3c2bc"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ddenysov/canope/commits/0c9dcbf7a6c4e3d7d715f024aee93a9961219c4a/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2025-06-05T19:57:29Z","email":"dmytro.denysov@danads.se","name":"Dmytro Denysov"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2025-06-05T19:57:29Z","email":"dmytro.denysov@danads.se","name":"Dmytro Denysov"},"message":"Initial commit","tree":{"sha":"4ad25a94543555147676399b4f617566cbd5556d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ddenysov/canope/gi